We suggest exploring the CP properties of the three-body decays $	au^{-} 	o 3p \pi^{-} 
u_{	au}$ through amplitude analysis. This investigation could provide significant insights, as the interference between different amplitudes in this process can result in substantial CP violation, far exceeding unitarity constraints. Furthermore, we propose using data from the BESIII collaboration to examine CP properties in $	au^{-} 	o 2p 2\pi^{-} 
u_{	au}$ using their recently released data. If a non-robust result is obtained from this analysis, future experiments are likely to yield more precise CP violation measurements in this decay. Additionally, we advocate for the investigation of $	au^{-} 	o 3p \pi^{-} 
u_{	au}$ using experimental data from fixed-target experiments such as the KLOE/KLOE2 and the BESIII collaborations as well as CERN's LHCb, as well as forthcoming experiments. We estimate that forthcoming experiments will be able to determine CP properties in this decay with a precision of better than $10^{-4}$. In addition to this, we outline a test to verify lepton flavor universality in the three-body decay $	au^{-} 	o 2p 2\pi^{-} 
u_{	au}$ that can be carried out with data from the BESIII collaboration. Finally, we examine the potential for the decay $	au^{-} 	o ho^{-} \pi^{-} 
u_{	au}$ to reveal signs of new physics due to its sensitivity to the transverse structure of the weak current, as well as determine the precision with which forthcoming experiments can measure CP properties in this decay. \end{abstract}
